## THE QUESTIONS

1. **THE ONE I MOST WANT CHECKED — I believe this detector has exactly ONE row that can see a
   WRONG-REGISTER edit, and it is the most fragile row in the suite.** `M5` swaps `inten` for
   `isteer` in the read *and* the write, and scores **25/26, killed only by `ANCHOR_EXC`**. My
   reading: every `D_*` row is a write-then-read-back round-trip through the same arm, so a
   CONSISTENT swap round-trips perfectly and is invisible to all of them by construction — the
   same-path round-trip blindness. Only `ANCHOR_EXC` observes through a channel OUTSIDE the arm
   (the interrupt controller reading `d->enable`, producing an IRQ at vector `0x18`).
   **Is that reading right?** And if so: `ANCHOR_EXC` is the free-running row with a breakpoint and a
   timeout — **the suite's single anchored row is also its most load-sensitive one.** Does this
   detector owe a second, cheaper anchored row? If yes, name a concrete one.
2. **CLAUSE (d) FOR A PURE DELETION.** The rule for a fix that adds no predicate is: re-introduce the
   removed behaviour under a DIFFERENT predicate — not the same one widened, which is (b) in (d)'s
   clothes. I count FOUR such mutants here (`M1`, `M_OPCODE2`, `M_RD15`, `M_NREAD`), all killed.
   **Is the enumeration COMPLETE?** The dominating predicates of the edited lines, walked up only to
   the enclosing function boundary, are `crm == 0`, `crn == 0`, `l_bit`. **Name any different-predicate
   reinstatement that would ESCAPE all 26 rows.** That is the ask-again, and it is capped at one.
3. **THE DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT SIDE.** The fix deletes `exit(` from a device-ish arm, so the detector
   owes a row per predicate that used to gate death, proving legal accesses still land. My reading:
   `crn` is covered by `SIB_READ` (crn 4), `crm` by `D_TRR0` (crm 1), and `l_bit` by the write-side
   round-trip rows. **Is the `l_bit` accept side genuinely pinned, or is it pinned only by rows that
   would also pass under M5?** If the latter, the accept row for the write path is vacuous under the
   very mutant that matters.
4. **IS MEMBER 4 (ACCEPT) THE RIGHT OPTION-SET MEMBER?** The alternatives are DECLINE (silent),
   DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE (`(once)`, the house idiom, ~82% of suppression markers), FAULT, BACKED
   ACCEPT, and IMPLEMENT. **Argue against my choice if you can.** Specifically: is there any reading
